SKILL_MD = """---
name: tiantasks
description: The user's task and issue queues (possibly shared with teammates), managed with the `tt` CLI. Use when the user mentions tasks, tickets, issues, todos, "the queue", "your queue" or Tiantasks; when asked to work on the next task or on assigned work; when you find a bug you won't fix right now; or when your work resolves an open item.
---

# Tiantasks

`tt` (also `tiantasks`) stores tasks (T-n) and issues (I-n) in a database outside the repo,
grouped by project (the git repo name by default). There is nothing to stage or commit. People
watch a live page (`tt ui` opens it), so your changes show up for them right away.

**Your identity:** run `tt whoami`. On a private board you are `claude`; on a shared board you
are `claude-<user>` (for example `claude-oliver`), so each person's Claude has its own queue.
`tt` fills this in automatically; `tt next` only hands you items assigned to you.

## Commands

| Command | Does |
|---|---|
| `tt list` · `tt list --for me` · `tt list -A` · `--json` | Open items in this project (`-A` = every project; `--all` includes resolved) |
| `tt next --start` | Take the top item assigned to you and mark it in progress; `--any` also takes unassigned work |
| `tt add "title" [-p crit\\|high\\|med\\|low] [-t tag] [-d "details"] [--to NAME]` | New task |
| `tt issue "title" [same flags]` | New issue / bug |
| `tt start ID` · `tt stop ID` | Mark in progress / back to open |
| `tt resolve ID [ID…] -m "what you did"` | Check it off (shown struck through) with a resolution note |
| `tt assign ID NAME` · `tt comment ID "text"` · `tt edit ID …` · `tt reopen ID` | Housekeeping |
| `tt show ID` | Full details and history |

## Workflow

1. **Asked to work the queue or "do your tasks":** run `tt next --start`, do the work, then
   `tt resolve <ID> -m "<one-line summary of the change>"`. Repeat until `tt next` comes back
   empty or the user says to stop.
2. **You find a bug, failing test or tech debt you're not fixing now:**
   `tt issue "<one-line summary>" -p <priority> -t <area> -d "<repro or file:line>"`.
3. **You identify follow-up work:** `tt add "<summary>"`. Only use `--to me` (assign yourself)
   if the user asked you to take it on. Assign teammates by name only when asked.
4. **You finish something that matches an open item** (check `tt list`): resolve it with a note.
5. Mention the IDs you opened or resolved in your reply.
6. If `tt` says it can't reach the shared board or the token was rejected, tell the user; don't
   fall back to editing anything by hand.

Keep titles to one line and put details in `-d` or `tt comment`. Use `--json` when you need to
parse the output.
"""

